name w2-osirak
time 1981-06-07T12:55:00Z
carry units ground stores
stop_on never

# CAMPAIGN W2 - OSIRAK 1981 (OPERATION OPERA): REACH, NOT COMBAT.
# Spec, anchor, sources and disputes: doc/campaigns/w2-osirak.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
#
# THE TENTH AND LAST OF THE TEN CAMPAIGNS TO BE BUILT, AND THE ONLY ONE WHOSE ANTAGONIST IS FUEL. Its
# own §2 contract: "the subject is REACH, not combat - eight of the ten missions have no air opposition
# at all. If a mission can be won by shooting somebody, it belongs in another campaign", and "every
# mission reports remaining `fuelLbs` at the last waypoint". Every sortie below does.
#
# WHY W2 IS BUILDABLE NOW AND WAS NOT WHEN ITS SPEC WAS WRITTEN. The spec listed FOUR of ten missions
# as runnable and its own honest headline read: "W2 is the campaign FlightBox is furthest from being
# able to fly ... this campaign's first deliverable is not a mission file, it is a drop-tank entry in
# core/FBStore.h and a boom." Rule 7 says a blocked mission is re-checked against the TREE and not
# against a gap's status line, and this is the ninth time that has been paid:
#   C5  HALF CLOSED 2026-07-30 - `tank370` is a catalogue row with `FuelLbs > 0`, it owns one of the
#       airframe's own JSBSim tanks while it hangs there, JSBSim's own priority empties it first, and
#       jettison takes its mass, its drag and its fuel in one call. `set fuel_int_pct` makes a CLEAN
#       jet declarable for the first time. THE BOOM IS NOT BUILT and is not approximated - see below
#   C8  BUILT 2026-07-28 - `mk84` is in the catalogue, so the campaign flies THE ANCHOR'S OWN WEAPON
#       and w2-01 asks a sharper question than the spec could
#   C1  CLOSED 2026-07-28 - nine ground positions that find, gate an envelope and fire out of a finite
#       magazine, four of them guns. w2-08 was called "UNANSWERABLE TODAY" by the spec and is answered
#   C22 CLOSED 2026-07-28 - a node that cues a fire unit's antenna over a command bus. w2-07 is built
#       on it, and on what it does NOT reach (below)
#   C2  CLOSED 2026-07-28 - all ten declare 1981-06-07T12:55:00Z, the anchor's own 15:55 local launch
#   C12 CLOSED 2026-07-28 / C0 CLOSED 2026-07-28 - the objective kinds and this file
#   AND THE ONE THE PILOT OWED: the BINGO branch was DEAD CODE until 2026-07-30 (doc/pilot.md §7.4a).
#       W3 measured it at seven of 184 columns and ZERO METRES. w2-09 is the campaign that asks the
#       question now that it can be reached.
#
# WHAT IS STILL MISSING, AND WHAT EACH SORTIE DOES ABOUT IT:
#   C5(b) NO AERIAL REFUELLING. Half of the campaign's own defining gap is still open, deliberately:
#       it is the expensive half (a tanker unit, a boom, a contact/latch state machine, a transfer
#       rate, a formation task) and W2 does not need it if tanks carry. WHAT STAYS UNMEASURABLE
#       BECAUSE OF IT, stated once for the whole campaign: nothing here says what a tanker would have
#       been worth; every range number is about an aircraft that launched full and never took another
#       pound; and the anchor's "tanks exhausted en route" is reproduced by CARRYING tanks that run
#       dry (MEASURED at 675.8 km on the war load), never by filling anything in flight
#   C20 NO TERRAIN-FOLLOWING GUIDANCE, and it is the reason ONE HALF OF A [DISPUTED] ANCHOR VALUE IS
#       NOT FLOWN. The ingress altitude is 30 m [T4] or 240 m [T4], a factor of eight, and the spec
#       says which question each answers. `Direct` holds an ALTITUDE ABOVE THE DATUM. The real ground
#       under this route was MEASURED off fb-tiles /elev - Etzion 523.59 m, 30.20/35.50 = 1,599.22 m,
#       30.50/36.50 = 896.99 m, 31.00/38.00 = 578.06 m, 31.50/39.50 = 865.62 m, 32.00/41.00 = 622.15 m,
#       32.50/42.50 = 301.25 m, 33.00/44.00 = 42.83 m, the dome 46.70 m - so a 30 m ASL leg over it is
#       up to 1,569 m UNDERGROUND. THE 30 m VARIANT IS NOT FLYABLE AND THE CAMPAIGN SAYS SO INSTEAD OF
#       WORKING AROUND IT. Both disputed values are carried; neither is preferred; the flown one is
#       240 m, over a 0 m plane, which is the only reason it is expressible at all
#   C10 NO DIVE AND NO POP-UP. Every release in all ten files is a LEVEL laydown at 240 m, not a 35 deg
#       dive releasing at 1,100 m out of a 2,100 m pop-up. w2-01 prices the difference
#   C15 NO TIME-ON-TARGET. w2-10's four pairs are spaced 1,029 m along track = 5.00 s at the commanded
#       400 kt, i.e. THE AUTHOR did the arithmetic. That is what `C15` means and the file says so
#   C4  NO TERRAIN MASKING - w2-07's "radar blind spot" has nothing to be blind behind, and every
#       detection range in this campaign is an upper bound
#   C6  the AIRBORNE half is untouched: A NODE CANNOT CUE A FIGHTER. w2-07 measures the consequence
#   C7  NO F-15 - the escort of w2-06 and w2-10 is a second F-16 flight, declared in both headers
#   C21 NO DECLARABLE INITIAL DAMAGE - w2-09's "already hit" dome stands intact, and says why
#   C17 ONE RUNWAY, NO DIVERT FIELD - a raid at the limit of range has no alternate to declare
#
# AND THE ONE THAT IS THIS CAMPAIGN'S ALONE: THE CARRY CANNOT CARRY W2'S SUBJECT. doc/missions/campaign.md
# refuses FUEL as a carried fact, with a stated reason ("a sortie starts from a base with a briefed
# load; carrying fuel across a landing models the GROUND time, which does not exist"). That reason is
# correct and the consequence is still that the one campaign whose antagonist is fuel is the one whose
# campaign layer is blind to it. Every W2 fuel number is therefore a WITHIN-SORTIE number.
#
# THE GROUND, ONCE, FOR ALL TEN: `--elev const`, a 0 m plane. Every fingerprint below is comparable
# ONLY against runs whose campaign-summary.txt records `elev const`. Passing it explicitly is a
# PRECONDITION and not a convention (O3's warning): fb-gym's OWN default is `--elev swiss` when the
# baked DEM is on disk, which validates an explicit spawn altitude against the resolved ground.
#
# THE ARENA IS THE ANCHOR'S OWN GEOGRAPHY, not a box: Etzion AB 29.94000 34.87000 to the Osirak
# reactor at Al-Tuwaitha 33.20000 44.52000 [T4, both approximate in the source and carried as given].
# Bearing 068.37 deg; great circle 982.9 km each way, 1,965.9 km out and back [DERIVED]. The anchor's
# own "over 1,600 km round trip" and "~1,090 km straight-line each way" do not agree with each other or
# with its own coordinates; ALL THREE ARE CARRIED AND NONE IS AVERAGED. Scale: 1 deg lat = 111,320 m,
# 1 deg lon at 31.570 N = 94,845 m [DERIVED].
#
# NO CATALOGUE AIRCRAFT ROW FLIES IN ANY W2 FILE, as in W3, W4, O3 and W1: `ACCEPTED` means "accepted
# as a FLIGHT MODEL" and every generated deck is `ALPHA`, which may not answer a campaign question.
# W2 needs none - the F-16C is the anchor's own type. THE SUBSTITUTIONS AND THEIR DIRECTIONS:
#   F-16A Netz 1981    -> the `f16` module (a Block-50-class jet with an F100-PW-229). SAME FAMILY,
#                         DIFFERENT BLOCK. Direction: the flown jet is more capable, so every range
#                         number here is a FlightBox number and never a claim about the 1981 aircraft
#   F-15A escort       -> a second F-16 FLIGHT (`C7`). Direction: WEAKER in radar and missile than the
#                         historical escort, and stronger than an `f15c` row that cannot fire at all
#                         (MEASURED by O5). Only two of the anchor's six are staged; the other four
#                         were a dispersed diversion with no geometry this tree could give them
#   Iraqi MiG-21/23/F1 -> the `mig29` module. Direction: MATERIALLY STRONGER than anything Iraq had in
#                         1981 - look-down radar, R-73, helmet cue. A bad Blue result says LESS than it
#                         seems to; a good one says more
#   Iraqi AAA          -> `zsu23` / `zu23` of the same designations. SAME TYPES
#
# CALLSIGN AND CARRY TOPOLOGY, STATED BEFORE THE FIRST MISSION FILE:
#   THE CHAIN IS 06 -> 10 AND IT CARRIES TWO CALLSIGNS, `w2esc1`/`w2esc2`, the escort pair. WHAT THAT
#   CHAIN CLAIMS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT: the raid flew ONE sortie, so unlike W1's kill removal this is
#   NOT the anchor's own procedure - it is the CAMPAIGN's experiment, "is an escort that has already
#   fought still an escort?", and it is declared as such rather than dressed up as history. Every other
#   sortie has pairwise disjoint callsigns, GROUND OBJECTS INCLUDED (O5's rule), which matters here
#   because eight of the ten attack ground objects.
#   CONTROLLED VARIANTS: 03 -> 04 (ONE fact: two Mk-84) -> 05 (ONE fact: two tank370); and
#   01 -> 08 (ONE fact: four anti-aircraft positions). A CHAIN STEP IS NEVER A CONTROLLED VARIANT:
#   06 is a control of nothing and 10 has no sibling. 02, 07 and 09 stand alone; 09 carries its own
#   control INSIDE it (two F-16s one line apart).
#
# WHAT THE SPEC ASKED FOR AND DID NOT GET, NAMED HERE RATHER THAN QUIETLY DROPPED. Nine of the spec's
# ten missions are built with their own tactical questions. ONE IS DROPPED:
#   spec 5 `w2-05-pair-pop`, "two-ship pop attack, 5 s spacing", asking whether two aircraft can be
#     sequenced onto one aim point five seconds apart without a time-on-target mechanism (`C15`).
#     NOT BUILT AS ITS OWN FILE: `C10` means there is no pop attack to fly, and the spacing half is
#     answered at full scale INSIDE the capstone, where four pairs are spaced by construction and the
#     releases come out 5.0 s apart. A separate two-ship would have measured the same arithmetic twice
# AND ONE SORTIE IS NEW, in that slot: `w2-05-tanks`, the second rung of the range ladder and the one
# the spec could not write because the tank did not exist. IT IS THE CAMPAIGN'S OWN SUBJECT.
#
# THE TEN SORTIES AND THE ONE THING EACH CHANGES - each file's own header carries its binding reading
# rule (doc/missions/INDEX.md rule 5) and this list is the campaign's index only:
#   01 dome        a level Mk-84 against the reactor dome            <- ctrl of 08
#   02 ingress     the 300 km low-level leg and the [DISPUTED] altitude
#   03 radius      the range ride, CLEAN                             <- ctrl of 04
#   04 loaded      [ctrl 03] ONE fact: two Mk-84                     <- ctrl of 05
#   05 tanks       [ctrl 04] ONE fact: two tank370. THE NEW SORTIE
#   06 escort      2 escort v 1 interceptor, strikers ahead of it     <- CHAIN HEAD
#   07 blindspot   the early-warning node, and the channel it has no receiver for
#   08 flak        [ctrl 01] ONE fact: four anti-aircraft positions
#   09 bingo       the minimum-fuel decision, with its own control inside the file
#   10 opera       the full raid, 8 + 2 v 2                           <- CHAIN TERMINUS
#
# SIX ATTRIBUTION RUNS ARE NOT PART OF THE TEN and live in doc/campaigns/w2-osirak.md §State:
#   A1  the low-level flameout ladder: clean / 2 x Mk-84 / 2 x tank370 / tanks dropped when dry /
#       the full war load - five straight legs at 240 m, 400 kt, `fuel_int_pct 100`, to fuel exhaustion
#   A2  the release ladder: w2-01 at 300/350/400/450 kt x 240/600/1,100 m, twelve points, aimErrM
#   A3  w2-02 with a 30 m spawn instead of 240 m - the other half of the [DISPUTED] value
#   A4  w2-02 under `--elev tiles` over the real Jordan/Iraq - what the ground would have done
#   A5  w2-10 STANDALONE - what the carried escort was worth
#   A6  the jettison probe: what happens when a bomb-carrying jet is told to let its tanks go
#
# READING RULE FOR THE CAMPAIGN. Most of these sorties are MEASURING RIGS and their exit code is not
# their verdict. Exit 2 on sortie 04 is the CORRECT outcome and it is the campaign's central negative
# result: a jet under the war load does not get home from a leg the clean jet does. THE CAMPAIGN EXIT
# IS THE WORST STEP'S. Read, per sortie and in this order: (1) `fuelLbs` in the last telemetry row,
# every time, (2) `stores DELIVERY aimErrM` against the 17.7 m the dome needs (derived in w2-01),
# (3) the ground `UNIT_RESULT` lines, (4) who is alive. Do NOT read an air-to-air outcome above two
# aircraft a side as a result at all - W1's saturation gate says it is a fixed point.

mission ../missions/w2-01-dome.fbm
mission ../missions/w2-02-ingress.fbm
mission ../missions/w2-03-radius.fbm
mission ../missions/w2-04-loaded.fbm
mission ../missions/w2-05-tanks.fbm
mission ../missions/w2-06-escort.fbm
mission ../missions/w2-07-blindspot.fbm
mission ../missions/w2-08-flak.fbm
mission ../missions/w2-09-bingo.fbm
mission ../missions/w2-10-opera.fbm
